cache_poisoning.recommendation:
1. Include all headers that influence the response in the cache key (`Vary: X-Forwarded-Host, X-Forwarded-Proto`), or better yet, do not use client-supplied forwarding headers to generate content.
2. Normalize and validate forwarding headers at the edge; discard client-supplied values.
3. Never cache authenticated responses or responses containing `Set-Cookie`; use `Cache-Control: private, no-store` for user-specific data.
4. Canonicalize URLs and query strings and reject unknown parameters; ensure the proxy and application use the same query parser.
5. Disable fat GET or include the request body in the cache key; restrict cacheable methods to GET and HEAD.
6. Apply cache deception protection: do not cache based on file extension alone; validate `Content-Type` and authentication state before caching.
7. Configure the CDN to strip hop-by-hop headers and never trust `X-Original-URL` or `X-Rewrite-URL`.
8. Example Nginx configuration:
```nginx
proxy_cache_key "$scheme$request_method$host$request_uri";
proxy_ignore_headers X-Accel-Expires;
proxy_hide_header X-Forwarded-Host;
add_header Vary "X-Forwarded-Host, X-Forwarded-Proto" always;
proxy_cache_bypass $http_authorization $cookie_session;
```
9. Monitor for cache poisoning using canary tokens and alert on responses containing external hostnames.

cors_misconfiguration.recommendation:
1. Use an explicit allowlist of exact origins (scheme + host + port); never use raw reflection or permissive regex.
2. Do not combine `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` with credentials; for public APIs without credentials, `*` is acceptable.
3. Reject `Origin: null` and unknown origins by responding without any CORS headers.
4. Validate the origin on the server after full URL parsing, comparing the exact host; do not use `startsWith`/`endsWith`.
5. Send `Vary: Origin` to prevent cache poisoning of CORS responses.
6. Limit methods and headers to the minimum required; do not expose `Authorization` to untrusted origins.
7. Prefer tokens in the `Authorization` header over cookies for cross-origin APIs; if cookies are used, require `SameSite=Lax/Strict` and a CSRF token.
8. Spring example:
```java
@Bean
CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration c = new CorsConfiguration();
    c.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("https://app.alvo.com", "https://admin.alvo.com"));
    c.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST"));
    c.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Content-Type", "Authorization"));
    c.setAllowCredentials(true);
    UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource s = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
    s.registerCorsConfiguration("/api/**", c);
    return s;
}
```
9. Monitor subdomains and remove orphaned DNS records to prevent bypass via subdomain takeover.

crlf_injection.recommendation:
1. Reject any input containing `\r`, `\n`, or control characters before inserting it into headers; use allowlist validation.
2. Use framework APIs that automatically encode header values; never concatenate raw strings into `Location`/`Set-Cookie`.
3. In Java, use `HttpServletResponse.setHeader()` with sanitized values and validate with regex; avoid `addHeader` with raw input.
4. In Nginx, use the normalized `$uri` variable and `return 302` with a validated destination; do not forward untrusted headers.
5. Normalize and reject encoded sequences (`%0d`, `%0a`, `%250d`) after URL decoding.
6. Configure proxies and WAFs to block CR/LF in headers and parameters, but do not rely on them as the sole defense.
7. Sanitize log output (escape newlines) and never use user input as a header name.
8. Java example:
```java
private static final Pattern CRLF = Pattern.compile("[\\r\\n\\u0000]");

public void redirect(HttpServletResponse res, String target) throws IOException {
    if (CRLF.matcher(target).find()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("invalid target");
    res.setStatus(302);
    res.setHeader("Location", target);
}
```
9. Test responses with `curl -i` and inspect headers and body after each input.

host_header_injection.recommendation:
1. Validate the `Host` header against an allowlist of canonical domains; reject unknown hosts with HTTP 400.
2. Do not trust `X-Forwarded-Host`/`X-Forwarded-Server` for URL construction; use static configuration (`app.base-url`) or the validated host.
3. Configure the reverse proxy to overwrite forwarding headers and not pass client-supplied values.
4. Generate absolute URLs from trusted configuration, not from the request.
5. For password resets, use single-use, short-lived tokens bound to the user; validate the host in the reset link.
6. Apply `Vary` and cache keys that include the canonical host; do not cache responses with a reflected Host.
7. Disable routing by `X-Original-URL`/`X-Rewrite-URL` at the edge.
8. Nginx example:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name alvo.com www.alvo.com;
    if ($host !~ ^(alvo\.com|www\.alvo\.com)$) { return 444; }
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host "";
}
```
9. Java/Spring example:
```java
private static final Set<String> ALLOWED = Set.of("alvo.com", "www.alvo.com");

@Component
public class HostFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {
        String host = req.getServerName();
        if (!ALLOWED.contains(host)) { res.sendError(400); return; }
        chain.doFilter(req, res);
    }
}
```
10. Monitor anomalous hosts, reset attempts with external Host values, and spikes in invalid tokens.

idor.recommendation:
1. Enforce authorization on every object access on the server side, verifying `owner_id` / `tenant_id` against the authenticated user. Do not rely on opaque IDs or frontend checks.
2. Apply deny-by-default: if the user does not have explicit permission, return 404 or 403.
3. Centralize authorization checks in a policy engine and cover all methods and formats (REST, GraphQL, mobile).
4. Use opaque, random identifiers (UUIDv4/ULID) to reduce enumeration; still validate ownership regardless.
5. In GraphQL, enforce authorization in every resolver and on `node(id:)` queries; do not expose administrative fields.
6. Protect export and download endpoints with ownership checks and short-lived signed URLs.
7. Audit access patterns and alert on enumeration indicators (high 403/404 rates, sequential ID scanning).
8. Spring example:
```java
@GetMapping("/api/invoices/{id}")
public Invoice getInvoice(@PathVariable Long id, @AuthenticationPrincipal User user) {
    Invoice invoice = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(NotFoundException::new);
    if (!invoice.getOwnerId().equals(user.getId()) && !user.hasRole("ADMIN")) {
        throw new AccessDeniedException("forbidden");
    }
    return invoice;
}
```
9. Write automated tests that attempt to access resources belonging to another user across all HTTP methods.

jwt_signature_bypass.recommendation:
1. Pin the expected algorithm per endpoint; never trust the token's `alg` claim. Explicitly reject `none`.
2. Use mature, up-to-date libraries (jjwt, Nimbus JOSE+JWT, Spring Security OAuth2 Resource Server) and validate `iss`, `aud`, `exp`, `nbf`, `iat`, `sub`.
3. For RS256/ES256, validate the signature with a fixed public key or a trusted JWKS by issuer; block client-controlled `jwk`, `jku`, `x5u`.
4. Sanitize `kid` with an allowlist of known key IDs; never use `kid` in SQL, shell commands, or file paths without validation.
5. Use HMAC secrets of at least 256 bits, stored in a KMS or Secret Manager, with regular rotation.
6. Prefer asymmetric algorithms (ES256/EdDSA) for inter-service tokens and validate `typ`/`cty`.
7. Implement revocation (denylist/`jti`), key rotation with `kid`, and short lifetimes for access tokens.
8. Spring Security example:
```java
@Bean
JwtDecoder jwtDecoder() {
    NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withPublicKey(publicKey).build();
    OAuth2TokenValidator<Jwt> withIssuer = JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer("https://issuer");
    decoder.setJwtValidator(withIssuer);
    return decoder;
}
```
9. Add regression tests that reject `alg:none`, HS256 with a public key, external `jku`, and invalid `kid`.

mass_assignment.recommendation:
1. Use operation-specific DTOs (Create/Update) with an explicit field allowlist; never bind the domain entity directly to the request body.
2. In Spring, use `@JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = false)` or `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES` to reject unknown fields; avoid `@ModelAttribute` on entities.
```java
public record UpdateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank String name,
    @Email String email
) {}
```
3. In Rails, use strong parameters (`params.require(:user).permit(:name, :email)`); in Node, validate with Zod/Joi and build the object field by field; in Laravel, use a restrictive `$fillable`.
4. Never allow the client to set `role`, `isAdmin`, `permissions`, `ownerId`, `tenantId`, `balance`, or `status`; these fields must be derived server-side.
5. Apply authorization and business rules on the server, regardless of what the client sends.
6. Reject unknown properties and return HTTP 400; do not silently ignore them.
7. Audit sensitive changes and alert on role/owner modifications outside the administrative workflow.
8. Cover all creation/update endpoints with tests that submit extra fields.

open_redirect.recommendation:
1. Do not accept arbitrary URLs. Use an allowlist of exact destinations or a server-side key map (`?next=dashboard`, `?next=billing`).
2. If accepting a URL is unavoidable, parse it with a URL library, require the `https` scheme, validate the host against an exact allowlist, and reject userinfo, fragments, and unexpected ports.
3. Prefer internal relative paths and validate that they start with `/` and not with `//` or `/\`.
4. Block `javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`, and non-HTTP(S) schemes.
5. Do not rely on `Referer` for destination decisions; validate the parameter explicitly.
6. In OAuth flows, validate `redirect_uri` by exact match and do not allow an internal open redirect on the callback endpoint.
7. Add an interstitial warning page ("You are leaving example.com") for legitimate external destinations.
8. Java example:
```java
private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_HOSTS = Set.of("app.alvo.com", "www.alvo.com");

public String safeRedirect(String raw) {
    URI uri = URI.create(raw);
    if (uri.getUserInfo() != null || uri.getFragment() != null) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    if (!"https".equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getScheme())) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    if (!ALLOWED_HOSTS.contains(uri.getHost())) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    return uri.toString();
}
```
9. Cover the flow with tests that attempt `//evil.com`, `%2f%2f`, `@`, backslash, and double encoding bypasses.

ssrf.recommendation:
1. Use a strict allowlist of permitted hosts, ports, and schemes; never accept arbitrary URLs.
2. Resolve DNS on the server, validate all returned IP addresses, and block private ranges, loopback, link-local, multicast, and metadata addresses. Re-validate after every redirect.
3. Disable automatic redirects or restrict them to the same origin/allowlist; validate each hop.
4. Pin the resolved IP (DNS pinning) and use the validated IP for the actual connection to prevent DNS rebinding.
5. Block non-HTTP(S) schemes (`file`, `gopher`, `dict`, `ftp`, `ldap`, `jar`) and unnecessary ports.
6. In cloud environments, enforce IMDSv2 (token required), restrict hop limit, and apply least privilege to IAM roles.
7. Segment the network: the service performing fetches should not be able to reach the control plane, databases, or metadata endpoints.
8. Use an egress proxy with allowlist, authentication, and logging; inspect responses and enforce size/timeout limits.
9. Java example:
```java
private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_HOSTS = Set.of("images.alvo.com", "cdn.alvo.com");

URI uri = URI.create(userUrl);
if (!"https".equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getScheme())) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
if (!ALLOWED_HOSTS.contains(uri.getHost())) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
InetAddress[] addrs = InetAddress.getAllByName(uri.getHost());
for (InetAddress a : addrs) {
    if (a.isLoopbackAddress() || a.isSiteLocalAddress() || a.isLinkLocalAddress()
        || a.isAnyLocalAddress() || a.isMulticastAddress()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException();
    }
}
HttpClient.newBuilder().followRedirects(HttpClient.Redirect.NEVER).build();
```
10. Monitor outbound requests targeting metadata endpoints and private IP ranges; alert on attempts.

xxe.recommendation:
1. Disable DTDs and external entities in the parser. In Java:
```java
DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl", true);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities", false);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-parameter-entities", false);
dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/nonvalidating/load-external-dtd", false);
dbf.setXIncludeAware(false);
dbf.setExpandEntityReferences(false);
```
2. For StAX/SAX, disable `external-general-entities`, `external-parameter-entities`, and `load-external-dtd`; set `XMLInputFactory.IS_SUPPORTING_EXTERNAL_ENTITIES = false`.
3. For Python, use `defusedxml`; for .NET, set `XmlReaderSettings.DtdProcessing = Prohibit` and `XmlResolver = null`.
4. Prefer JSON when possible; if XML is required, validate against an XSD and reject any `DOCTYPE` declaration.
5. Limit payload size, nesting depth, and entity expansion count; configure parser timeout and memory limits.
6. For file uploads, validate the actual content type, disable XML processing in SVG/DOCX, and use a sandboxed converter.
7. Isolate the XML parser in a separate service without filesystem or network access when processing untrusted input.
8. Monitor for payloads containing `DOCTYPE`, `SYSTEM`, `PUBLIC`, `file://`, and out-of-band callbacks.
